/math-academy-id "q-185191" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{9}{2}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ (-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{2}) & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\cdot 9}{2\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8014c3df-88df-588f-9988-d74a862c708c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8014c3df-88df-588f-9988-d74a862c708c" :answer-field/id #uuid "8014c3df-88df-588f-9988-d74a862c708c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b1532522-099e-56ec-9764-1cc17c4b430e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1532522-099e-56ec-9764-1cc17c4b430e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1532522-099e-56ec-9764-1cc17c4b430e" :answer/id #uuid "b1532522-099e-56ec-9764-1cc17c4b430e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{81}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-397f8e4c-8a02-58fc-a48a-6b5a482557f8" :question/id #uuid "397f8e4c-8a02-58fc-a48a-6b5a482557f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-185186" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{9}{7})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-\\frac{9}{7})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{9}{7}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{9}{7})}^{2} & = \\\\ (-\\frac{9}{7})\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{7}) & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\cdot 9}{7\\cdot 7} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{49} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72380fe8-b5f4-5ca0-9fd3-c403c8836a0a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72380fe8-b5f4-5ca0-9fd3-c403c8836a0a" :answer-field/id #uuid "72380fe8-b5f4-5ca0-9fd3-c403c8836a0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cc3e59fe-fbeb-561a-9571-4860f2729517" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc3e59fe-fbeb-561a-9571-4860f2729517"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc3e59fe-fbeb-561a-9571-4860f2729517" :answer/id #uuid "cc3e59fe-fbeb-561a-9571-4860f2729517" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{81}{49}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-63757b4a-7f76-56b7-8980-04eae99b6886" :question/id #uuid "63757b4a-7f76-56b7-8980-04eae99b6886" :question/math-academy-id "q-185112" :question/problem "$(-4)^{2}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To work out $(-4)^{2},$ we multiply $-4$ by itself:\n\n$$\n(-4)^{2}=(-4)\\cdot (-4)=16\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d310f33-d338-5c42-ab54-4e270186efcf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d310f33-d338-5c42-ab54-4e270186efcf" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d310f33-d338-5c42-ab54-4e270186efcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a97ad2c9-d76c-5dd5-894b-107c08fa063a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a97ad2c9-d76c-5dd5-894b-107c08fa063a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a97ad2c9-d76c-5dd5-894b-107c08fa063a" :answer/id #uuid "a97ad2c9-d76c-5dd5-894b-107c08fa063a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f5d1c8d9-881c-5cab-bdcb-86d5598dd1ea" :question/id #uuid "f5d1c8d9-881c-5cab-bdcb-86d5598dd1ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-99691" :question/problem "$(-14)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Since the product of two negative numbers gives a positive number, we have\n\n$$\n(-14)^{2}=(-14)\\cdot (-14)=14\\cdot 14\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $14\\cdot 14:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-14)^{2}={14}^{2}=196.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60d476c8-e088-547a-90fb-077d050bf5a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60d476c8-e088-547a-90fb-077d050bf5a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "60d476c8-e088-547a-90fb-077d050bf5a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e55ed20-0f49-5180-9a2e-e5d01e849230" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f387657e-c19c-508d-bc12-1bb030523224" "answer-82095f44-1913-591e-9d6f-be4c444fdb70" "answer-1e55ed20-0f49-5180-9a2e-e5d01e849230" "answer-d90a87cb-27d7-58cc-bcb2-fedd8c803b5c" "answer-8002aaa4-3721-5733-905a-d50f345d2225"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f387657e-c19c-508d-bc12-1bb030523224" :answer/id #uuid "f387657e-c19c-508d-bc12-1bb030523224" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-196" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82095f44-1913-591e-9d6f-be4c444fdb70" :answer/id #uuid "82095f44-1913-591e-9d6f-be4c444fdb70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e55ed20-0f49-5180-9a2e-e5d01e849230" :answer/id #uuid "1e55ed20-0f49-5180-9a2e-e5d01e849230" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "196" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d90a87cb-27d7-58cc-bcb2-fedd8c803b5c" :answer/id #uuid "d90a87cb-27d7-58cc-bcb2-fedd8c803b5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8002aaa4-3721-5733-905a-d50f345d2225" :answer/id #uuid "8002aaa4-3721-5733-905a-d50f345d2225" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 196" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-668fdcb5-56eb-5d4c-8344-17b223fde624" :question/id #uuid "668fdcb5-56eb-5d4c-8344-17b223fde624" :question/math-academy-id "q-185174" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction, ${(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To work out ${(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{3}{4}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =(-\\frac{3}{4})\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08d5d653-07e3-59f1-8358-9ff8a319ad03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08d5d653-07e3-59f1-8358-9ff8a319ad03" :answer-field/id #uuid "08d5d653-07e3-59f1-8358-9ff8a319ad03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7a5f45b0-7cff-5b5e-ac32-823e982d8ce7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a5f45b0-7cff-5b5e-ac32-823e982d8ce7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a5f45b0-7cff-5b5e-ac32-823e982d8ce7" :answer/id #uuid "7a5f45b0-7cff-5b5e-ac32-823e982d8ce7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-28d7dd84-346e-5b73-9ead-af1c62abb54d" :question/id #uuid "28d7dd84-346e-5b73-9ead-af1c62abb54d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104534" :question/problem "$(-3.2)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $-3.2$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-3.2)^{2}=(-3.2)\\cdot (-3.2)=(3.2)\\cdot (3.2)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $32\\times 32=1\\,024.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($3.2$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($3.2$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $1\\,024$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{24}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${3.2}^{2}=10.24.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7efa277c-8af9-5a93-b5aa-5d04f07f829a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7efa277c-8af9-5a93-b5aa-5d04f07f829a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7efa277c-8af9-5a93-b5aa-5d04f07f829a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2447b23c-518a-583f-94a7-4b1dc6ebfd02" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85877040-083d-56d8-9695-f3c5224bf53b" "answer-7fe427d5-a320-59d9-867c-71f5d00edbef" "answer-9e740911-a953-5ae2-bda7-47f9775a596a" "answer-2447b23c-518a-583f-94a7-4b1dc6ebfd02" "answer-64f6dd86-cc7d-5d80-b8e5-879b012c649d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85877040-083d-56d8-9695-f3c5224bf53b" :answer/id #uuid "85877040-083d-56d8-9695-f3c5224bf53b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10.04" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fe427d5-a320-59d9-867c-71f5d00edbef" :answer/id #uuid "7fe427d5-a320-59d9-867c-71f5d00edbef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10.24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e740911-a953-5ae2-bda7-47f9775a596a" :answer/id #uuid "9e740911-a953-5ae2-bda7-47f9775a596a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2447b23c-518a-583f-94a7-4b1dc6ebfd02" :answer/id #uuid "2447b23c-518a-583f-94a7-4b1dc6ebfd02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64f6dd86-cc7d-5d80-b8e5-879b012c649d" :answer/id #uuid "64f6dd86-cc7d-5d80-b8e5-879b012c649d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f176b794-be06-5dbc-b5c3-35aaeec4668c" :question/id #uuid "f176b794-be06-5dbc-b5c3-35aaeec4668c" :question/math-academy-id "q-69409" :question/problem "${1.6}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $1.6$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{1.6}^{2}=(1.6)\\cdot (1.6)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16\\times 16=256.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.6$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.6$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $256$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{56}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${1.6}^{2}=2.56\\,.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8fcffc96-726d-51dd-89e9-859a58d88bc6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8fcffc96-726d-51dd-89e9-859a58d88bc6" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fcffc96-726d-51dd-89e9-859a58d88bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b840462d-8d97-5643-baa8-700fcff510ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8b00607-3d6c-5a89-bdc6-d6456d75846e" "answer-b840462d-8d97-5643-baa8-700fcff510ba" "answer-820db4f9-0272-5520-8ff5-1246c0642b95" "answer-646f6c14-3b9e-545a-b130-288d60986d5d" "answer-9883503f-c186-5adb-8b1a-3be60665a2a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8b00607-3d6c-5a89-bdc6-d6456d75846e" :answer/id #uuid "e8b00607-3d6c-5a89-bdc6-d6456d75846e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b840462d-8d97-5643-baa8-700fcff510ba" :answer/id #uuid "b840462d-8d97-5643-baa8-700fcff510ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.56" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-820db4f9-0272-5520-8ff5-1246c0642b95" :answer/id #uuid "820db4f9-0272-5520-8ff5-1246c0642b95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.84" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-646f6c14-3b9e-545a-b130-288d60986d5d" :answer/id #uuid "646f6c14-3b9e-545a-b130-288d60986d5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9883503f-c186-5adb-8b1a-3be60665a2a1" :answer/id #uuid "9883503f-c186-5adb-8b1a-3be60665a2a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5bedd10-8ffa-5ad9-bdb2-5aa9ca8ef32b" :question/id #uuid "c5bedd10-8ffa-5ad9-bdb2-5aa9ca8ef32b" :question/math-academy-id "q-185204" :question/problem "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(5.3)^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $5.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{2}=(5.3)\\cdot (5.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $53\\times 53=2\\,809.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($5.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($5.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,809$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n28\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{09}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${5.3}^{2}=28.09\\,.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d0c6a72-5c4b-569d-8557-e438dfaa81e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d0c6a72-5c4b-569d-8557-e438dfaa81e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d0c6a72-5c4b-569d-8557-e438dfaa81e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ebb2e927-316a-5479-8921-68cb685d1107" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ebb2e927-316a-5479-8921-68cb685d1107"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebb2e927-316a-5479-8921-68cb685d1107" :answer/id #uuid "ebb2e927-316a-5479-8921-68cb685d1107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28.09" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-58bd0ac9-9ea6-5ce8-9fc3-fc8a73b17344" :question/id #uuid "58bd0ac9-9ea6-5ce8-9fc3-fc8a73b17344" :question/math-academy-id "q-171165" :question/problem "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-1.4)^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $(-1.4)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-1.4)^{2}=(-1.4)\\cdot (-1.4)=(1.4)\\cdot (1.4)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14\\times 14=196.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.4$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.4$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $196$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{96}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-1.4)^{2}=1.96\\,.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c7bab6b-4259-5022-a929-b79ac90b204a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c7bab6b-4259-5022-a929-b79ac90b204a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c7bab6b-4259-5022-a929-b79ac90b204a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2bf41ab3-6854-52f9-af7b-9e645e613508" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2bf41ab3-6854-52f9-af7b-9e645e613508"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bf41ab3-6854-52f9-af7b-9e645e613508" :answer/id #uuid "2bf41ab3-6854-52f9-af7b-9e645e613508" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e04ccdd3-f28d-58f9-a502-15fb5e93205e" :question/id #uuid "e04ccdd3-f28d-58f9-a502-15fb5e93205e" :question/math-academy-id "q-185196" :question/problem "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-0.3)^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $-0.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-0.3)^{2}=(-0.3)\\cdot (-0.3)=(0.3)\\cdot (0.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\times 3=9.\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($0.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($0.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $9$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{09}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-0.3)^{2}=0.09\\,.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c316753b-96b3-5e65-8e5d-5fdec3bbcfd0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c316753b-96b3-5e65-8e5d-5fdec3bbcfd0" :answer-field/id #uuid "c316753b-96b3-5e65-8e5d-5fdec3bbcfd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-382ab701-ec1b-571c-9078-66310f103b5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-382ab701-ec1b-571c-9078-66310f103b5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-382ab701-ec1b-571c-9078-66310f103b5f" :answer/id #uuid "382ab701-ec1b-571c-9078-66310f103b5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.09" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135180 :tutorial/content "To **square** a number means to multiply that number by itself. \n\nFor example, to \"square $3$\", we need to multiply $3$ by $3.$ We write the \"square of $3$\" as $3^2,$ so we have\n\n$$\n3^2 = 3 \\cdot 3 = {\\color{blue}9}.\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the square of $3$ is $9$\". We could also say \"$3$ squared equals $9$\".\n\nThe number $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression $3^{\\color{blue}2}$ is called the **power** or **exponent**.\n\nThe act of squaring a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $3$ with the following collection of three items:\n\n![](images/4a/4a4dbb46401052829138bddc1653ae843babf4c5cfb6ba9fc40ca29ede99bd70.png)\n\nThen we can picture $3^2$ by creating a square with length and height both equal to $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png)\n\nThere are $9$ items in total in our square, indicating that $3^2 = 9.$\n\nThe first few basic squares are shown below:\n\n| $1^2=1 \\cdot 1={\\color{blue}1}$ | $2^2=2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}4}$ | $3^2=3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}9}$ | $4^2=4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}16}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/ce/ce5db07809eb56891f31ac7fb0257edb42bd650f00c51c25857b02d1333165ad.png) | ![](images/61/6113e94a28fc50c7f1fe77ddf082d28d543aa9e2b6e65f82e8623a89f624f7db.png) | ![](images/cc/cc343eafb463e25ab429bafbc06216dad73037e5a02a2be9adfe046629636013.png) | ![](images/7d/7d56b21746fa8a0053fa32b6c2bc6d9393e13459cb65473e64589cc2f6a01ad3.png) |\n\nThe squares of our usual counting numbers are called **square numbers** or **perfect squares**. We can write them as follows:\n\n$$\n1^2, 2^2, 3^2, 4^2, 5^2, 6^2, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}4}, {\\color{blue}9}, {\\color{blue}16}, {\\color{blue}25}, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135181 :tutorial/content "The squares of negative numbers are always positive because a negative times a negative is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome squares of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}\\,=\\,(-1)\\cdot (-1) & \\,=\\,1 \\\\ (-2)^{2}\\,=\\,(-2)\\cdot (-2) & \\,=\\,4 \\\\ (-3)^{2}\\,=\\,(-3)\\cdot (-3) & \\,=\\,9 \\\\ (-4)^{2}\\,=\\,(-4)\\cdot (-4) & \\,=\\,16 \\\\ (-5)^{2}\\,=\\,(-5)\\cdot (-5) & \\,=\\,25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** When squaring negative numbers, the parentheses are important! \n\nConsider the following squared quantity:\n\n$$\n-3^2\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as $(-3)^2$. Instead, this is a shorthand way of writing\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot 3^2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the order of operations, we should square $3$ *first* and *then* multiply by $-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{3}^{2} & =(-1)\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134034 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
